The most common project question. Hit an API, render a list, let the user narrow it.

## What they're testing

Async data handling, derived state, debouncing, and whether you remember that requests can fail and results can be empty.

## Reference implementation

```javascript
import { useState, useEffect, useMemo } from 'react';

const API = 'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users';

export default function UserSearch() {
  const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);
  const [status, setStatus] = useState('loading'); // loading | error | ready
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [debouncedQuery, setDebouncedQuery] = useState('');
  const [role, setRole] = useState('all');

  // Debounce the query so filtering (or refetching) doesn't run per keystroke.
  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setTimeout(() => setDebouncedQuery(query), 300);
    return () => clearTimeout(timer);
  }, [query]);

  useEffect(() => {
    const controller = new AbortController();

    async function load() {
      setStatus('loading');
      try {
        const res = await fetch(API, { signal: controller.signal });
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error('HTTP ' + res.status);
        const data = await res.json();
        setUsers(data);
        setStatus('ready');
      } catch (err) {
        // An aborted request is not a real failure — the component moved on.
        if (err.name === 'AbortError') return;
        setStatus('error');
      }
    }

    load();
    return () => controller.abort();
  }, []);

  // Derived, not stored. Storing filtered results invites them to go stale.
  const visible = useMemo(() => {
    const q = debouncedQuery.trim().toLowerCase();
    return users.filter((user) => {
      const matchesQuery =
        !q ||
        user.name.toLowerCase().includes(q) ||
        user.email.toLowerCase().includes(q);
      const matchesRole = role === 'all' || user.role === role;
      return matchesQuery && matchesRole;
    });
  }, [users, debouncedQuery, role]);

  if (status === 'loading') return <p data-testid="loading">Loading…</p>;
  if (status === 'error') return <p data-testid="error">Something went wrong. Try again.</p>;

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        type="search"
        value={query}
        onChange={(e) => setQuery(e.target.value)}
        placeholder="Search users"
        aria-label="Search users"
        data-testid="search-input"
      />

      <select value={role} onChange={(e) => setRole(e.target.value)} aria-label="Filter by role">
        <option value="all">All roles</option>
        <option value="admin">Admin</option>
        <option value="member">Member</option>
      </select>

      {visible.length === 0 ? (
        <p data-testid="empty">No users match that search.</p>
      ) : (
        <ul data-testid="user-list">
          {visible.map((user) => (
            <li key={user.id} className="user-row">
              <strong>{user.name}</strong> — {user.email}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## The decisions worth understanding

**One `status` string beats three booleans.** `isLoading`, `isError`, `hasData` can contradict each other. A single state machine value can't.

**Filter derived with `useMemo`, never stored in state.** The classic bug is `const [filtered, setFiltered] = useState([])` plus an effect to keep it in sync — now you have two sources of truth and a render where they disagree.

**Debounce the value, not the handler.** Debouncing state with a `setTimeout` in an effect is fewer lines than wiring a debounced callback, and it survives re-renders correctly. If the prompt wants server-side search, put `debouncedQuery` in the fetch effect's dependency array instead of filtering locally.

**`AbortController` matters when the fetch depends on input.** With a static URL it's tidiness. With `[debouncedQuery]` in the deps it's correctness — otherwise a slow early response can land after a fast later one and overwrite it.

## Mistakes that cost points

- No empty state — the most-skipped requirement on the test.
- Debouncing but forgetting `clearTimeout` in the cleanup, so every keystroke leaves a live timer.
- `key={index}` on a list that filters and reorders. See [Reconciliation](/react-guide/4).
- Treating `AbortError` as a real error and flashing an error screen on every keystroke.
- Forgetting `res.ok` — `fetch` does not reject on 404 or 500.

