Given an array of records, render a table you can sort by column and page through.

## What they're testing

Comparator logic, off-by-one arithmetic, and whether interacting with one control corrupts the other (sorting while on page 3 is the classic trap).

## Reference implementation

```javascript
import { useState, useMemo } from 'react';

const COLUMNS = [
  { key: 'name', label: 'Name' },
  { key: 'age', label: 'Age' },
  { key: 'city', label: 'City' },
];

export default function DataTable({ rows }) {
  const [sortKey, setSortKey] = useState('name');
  const [sortDir, setSortDir] = useState('asc');
  const [page, setPage] = useState(1);
  const [pageSize, setPageSize] = useState(10);

  const sorted = useMemo(() => {
    // Copy first — .sort() mutates, and mutating props is a real bug.
    return [...rows].sort((a, b) => {
      const av = a[sortKey];
      const bv = b[sortKey];
      let cmp;
      if (typeof av === 'number' && typeof bv === 'number') {
        cmp = av - bv;
      } else {
        cmp = String(av).localeCompare(String(bv));
      }
      return sortDir === 'asc' ? cmp : -cmp;
    });
  }, [rows, sortKey, sortDir]);

  const totalPages = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(sorted.length / pageSize));
  // Clamp during render rather than syncing with an effect — no extra paint.
  const safePage = Math.min(page, totalPages);
  const start = (safePage - 1) * pageSize;
  const visible = sorted.slice(start, start + pageSize);

  function handleSort(key) {
    if (key === sortKey) {
      setSortDir((d) => (d === 'asc' ? 'desc' : 'asc'));
    } else {
      setSortKey(key);
      setSortDir('asc');
    }
    setPage(1); // Sorting invalidates your page position.
  }

  if (rows.length === 0) return <p data-testid="empty">No records to display.</p>;

  return (
    <div>
      <table data-testid="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            {COLUMNS.map((col) => (
              <th
                key={col.key}
                onClick={() => handleSort(col.key)}
                aria-sort={
                  sortKey === col.key
                    ? sortDir === 'asc' ? 'ascending' : 'descending'
                    : 'none'
                }
                style={{ cursor: 'pointer' }}
              >
                {col.label}
                {sortKey === col.key ? (sortDir === 'asc' ? ' ▲' : ' ▼') : ''}
              </th>
            ))}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {visible.map((row) => (
            <tr key={row.id} className="table-row">
              {COLUMNS.map((col) => (
                <td key={col.key}>{row[col.key]}</td>
              ))}
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div className="pagination">
        <button onClick={() => setPage((p) => p - 1)} disabled={safePage === 1}>
          Previous
        </button>
        <span data-testid="page-info">
          Page {safePage} of {totalPages}
        </span>
        <button onClick={() => setPage((p) => p + 1)} disabled={safePage === totalPages}>
          Next
        </button>

        <select
          value={pageSize}
          onChange={(e) => { setPageSize(Number(e.target.value)); setPage(1); }}
          aria-label="Rows per page"
        >
          {[10, 25, 50].map((n) => <option key={n} value={n}>{n} per page</option>)}
        </select>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## The decisions worth understanding

**`[...rows].sort()`, always.** `Array.prototype.sort` mutates in place. Sorting a prop array directly mutates the parent's data, and because the reference didn't change, React may not even re-render to show it. This is a nasty, hard-to-spot bug under time pressure.

**Clamp the page during render, don't sync it in an effect.** `Math.min(page, totalPages)` is one line and correct immediately. An effect that calls `setPage` when the page goes out of range renders once with a broken value first.

**Reset to page 1 on any change to the result set.** Sorting, filtering, changing page size — all of them make your current page number meaningless.

**Numbers subtract, strings `localeCompare`.** `String(10) < String(9)` is true, so comparing numbers as strings silently produces wrong order. Branch on type.

## Mistakes that cost points

- Sorting the prop array in place.
- Staying on page 5 after a filter leaves 3 results — user sees a blank table.
- `(page - 1) * pageSize` written as `page * pageSize`, skipping the first page of data.
- Non-clickable headers when the prompt said sortable columns.
- No `aria-sort` — cheap accessibility credit that graders sometimes check.

