Controlled inputs, per-field error messages, submit gating.

## What they're testing

Controlled-component discipline, validation timing (don't scream at someone who hasn't typed yet), and accessible error association.

## Reference implementation

```javascript
import { useState } from 'react';

const INITIAL = { name: '', email: '', password: '', age: '' };

function validate(values) {
  const errors = {};

  if (!values.name.trim()) {
    errors.name = 'Name is required';
  }

  if (!values.email.trim()) {
    errors.email = 'Email is required';
  } else if (!/^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(values.email)) {
    errors.email = 'Enter a valid email address';
  }

  if (values.password.length < 8) {
    errors.password = 'Password must be at least 8 characters';
  }

  if (values.age && (Number(values.age) < 18 || Number(values.age) > 120)) {
    errors.age = 'Age must be between 18 and 120';
  }

  return errors;
}

export default function SignupForm({ onSubmit }) {
  const [values, setValues] = useState(INITIAL);
  const [touched, setTouched] = useState({});
  const [submitted, setSubmitted] = useState(false);

  const errors = validate(values);
  const isValid = Object.keys(errors).length === 0;

  // One handler for every field — relies on the input's name attribute.
  function handleChange(e) {
    const { name, value } = e.target;
    setValues((prev) => ({ ...prev, [name]: value }));
  }

  function handleBlur(e) {
    setTouched((prev) => ({ ...prev, [e.target.name]: true }));
  }

  function handleSubmit(e) {
    e.preventDefault(); // Without this the page reloads and you lose everything.
    setSubmitted(true);
    if (!isValid) return;
    onSubmit(values);
    setValues(INITIAL);
    setTouched({});
    setSubmitted(false);
  }

  // Show an error only once the user has engaged with that field, or on submit.
  const showError = (field) => (touched[field] || submitted) && errors[field];

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit} noValidate data-testid="signup-form">
      {['name', 'email', 'password', 'age'].map((field) => (
        <div key={field} className="field">
          <label htmlFor={field}>{field[0].toUpperCase() + field.slice(1)}</label>
          <input
            id={field}
            name={field}
            type={field === 'password' ? 'password' : field === 'age' ? 'number' : 'text'}
            value={values[field]}
            onChange={handleChange}
            onBlur={handleBlur}
            aria-invalid={showError(field) ? 'true' : 'false'}
            aria-describedby={showError(field) ? field + '-error' : undefined}
          />
          {showError(field) && (
            <span id={field + '-error'} role="alert" className="error">
              {errors[field]}
            </span>
          )}
        </div>
      ))}

      <button type="submit" disabled={!isValid} data-testid="submit-btn">
        Sign up
      </button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

## The decisions worth understanding

**Validate on every render, don't store errors in state.** `const errors = validate(values)` is always correct by construction. Keeping errors in state means keeping them in sync, and they will drift.

**Separate "is it invalid" from "should we say so."** `errors` is computed immediately; `touched` and `submitted` decide visibility. Without that split, the form shows four red messages before the user types a character.

**One `handleChange` for all fields.** `[name]: value` with a computed key handles any number of inputs. Writing `handleNameChange`, `handleEmailChange`… wastes minutes you need elsewhere.

**`e.preventDefault()` on submit.** Forget it and the browser does a full page navigation. Under time pressure this reads as "my app randomly resets" and can eat ten minutes of debugging.

**Functional `setValues`.** `setValues(prev => ...)` rather than `setValues({...values, ...})` — safe under batching, and it's the habit that avoids [stale closures](/react-guide/7).

## Mistakes that cost points

- Missing `preventDefault`.
- `value` without `onChange` — React logs a warning and the input is frozen. See [Controlled vs Uncontrolled](/react-guide/22).
- Errors visible on first paint.
- Submit button never disabled when the prompt asked for it.
- No `htmlFor` / `id` pairing — clicking the label does nothing, and it's easy a11y credit.

