Cart, todo list, star rating, accordion, tabs, autocomplete. Provided data or none; all the interest is in state transitions.

## What they're testing

Whether you keep one source of truth and derive the rest, or scatter duplicated state that drifts apart.

## Reference implementation — shopping cart

```javascript
import { useState, useMemo } from 'react';

export default function Cart({ products }) {
  // One source of truth: a map of productId -> quantity.
  const [quantities, setQuantities] = useState({});

  const items = useMemo(
    () =>
      products
        .filter((p) => quantities[p.id] > 0)
        .map((p) => ({ ...p, qty: quantities[p.id], lineTotal: p.price * quantities[p.id] })),
    [products, quantities]
  );

  // Everything below is derived. Nothing here lives in state.
  const subtotal = useMemo(() => items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.lineTotal, 0), [items]);
  const itemCount = useMemo(() => items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.qty, 0), [items]);
  const tax = subtotal * 0.08;
  const total = subtotal + tax;

  function setQty(id, qty) {
    setQuantities((prev) => {
      if (qty <= 0) {
        const { [id]: _removed, ...rest } = prev;
        return rest;
      }
      return { ...prev, [id]: qty };
    });
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <ul data-testid="product-list">
        {products.map((p) => (
          <li key={p.id} className="product-row">
            <span>{p.name}</span>
            <span>{p.price.toFixed(2)}</span>
            <button onClick={() => setQty(p.id, (quantities[p.id] ?? 0) + 1)}>
              Add to cart
            </button>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <h2>Cart ({itemCount})</h2>

      {items.length === 0 ? (
        <p data-testid="empty-cart">Your cart is empty.</p>
      ) : (
        <>
          <ul data-testid="cart-items">
            {items.map((item) => (
              <li key={item.id} className="cart-row">
                {item.name}
                <button onClick={() => setQty(item.id, item.qty - 1)}>−</button>
                <span data-testid={'qty-' + item.id}>{item.qty}</span>
                <button onClick={() => setQty(item.id, item.qty + 1)}>+</button>
                <span>{item.lineTotal.toFixed(2)}</span>
                <button onClick={() => setQty(item.id, 0)}>Remove</button>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>

          <dl className="totals">
            <dt>Subtotal</dt><dd data-testid="subtotal">{subtotal.toFixed(2)}</dd>
            <dt>Tax</dt><dd>{tax.toFixed(2)}</dd>
            <dt>Total</dt><dd data-testid="total">{total.toFixed(2)}</dd>
          </dl>
        </>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## The core principle

**Store the minimum; derive everything else.**

The tempting version keeps `cartItems`, `subtotal`, `total`, and `itemCount` all in state, updated by hand on every action. Miss one update path and the total silently disagrees with the items. Here, one `quantities` object is authoritative and the four displayed values can't drift because they're recomputed from it.

Ask of every piece of state: *can I compute this from something else I already have?* If yes, compute it.

## Variants and their one trick each

| Widget | The trick |
|---|---|
| **Todo list** | Store the array; derive active/completed counts and the filtered view. Give each todo a stable id (`crypto.randomUUID()` or a counter), never the index. |
| **Star rating** | Two pieces of state: `rating` (committed) and `hover` (transient). Display `hover ?? rating`. |
| **Accordion** | Store `openId` (single-open) or a `Set` of open ids (multi-open). Not a boolean per panel. |
| **Tabs** | Store `activeTab` id. Render panels with `hidden` rather than unmounting if state must survive switching. |
| **Autocomplete** | `query`, `activeIndex`, `isOpen`. Debounce the query, and wire arrow keys / Enter / Escape — that's usually where the points are. |

## Mistakes that cost points

- Derived values duplicated into state, then out of sync.
- Mutating state directly — `items.push(x)` then `setItems(items)`. Same reference, no re-render.
- `key={index}` on a list with removal. Delete the second of three items and React reuses the wrong DOM node.
- Quantity allowed to go to 0 or negative without removing the row.
- Floating-point money displayed raw: `0.1 + 0.2` renders as `0.30000000000000004`. Use `.toFixed(2)`.

