Countdown, stopwatch, auto-advancing carousel, polling. Small surface, and the highest density of subtle bugs of any archetype.

## What they're testing

`useEffect` cleanup and the stale closure. This archetype exists specifically to catch people who don't understand either.

## Reference implementation — stopwatch

```javascript
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

export default function Stopwatch() {
  const [elapsed, setElapsed] = useState(0); // centiseconds
  const [running, setRunning] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!running) return;

    const id = setInterval(() => {
      // Functional update: never reads a captured 'elapsed', so it can't go stale.
      setElapsed((prev) => prev + 1);
    }, 10);

    return () => clearInterval(id); // Runs on pause AND on unmount.
  }, [running]);

  const format = (cs) => {
    const m = String(Math.floor(cs / 6000)).padStart(2, '0');
    const s = String(Math.floor((cs % 6000) / 100)).padStart(2, '0');
    const hundredths = String(cs % 100).padStart(2, '0');
    return m + ':' + s + '.' + hundredths;
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <output data-testid="display">{format(elapsed)}</output>
      <button onClick={() => setRunning((r) => !r)}>{running ? 'Pause' : 'Start'}</button>
      <button onClick={() => { setRunning(false); setElapsed(0); }}>Reset</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Reference implementation — auto-advancing carousel

```javascript
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

export default function Carousel({ slides, interval = 3000 }) {
  const [index, setIndex] = useState(0);
  const [paused, setPaused] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (paused || slides.length <= 1) return;

    const id = setInterval(() => {
      setIndex((prev) => (prev + 1) % slides.length); // modulo wraps to 0
    }, interval);

    return () => clearInterval(id);
  }, [paused, interval, slides.length]);

  if (slides.length === 0) return <p data-testid="empty">No slides.</p>;

  const go = (next) => setIndex((next + slides.length) % slides.length);

  return (
    <div
      className="carousel"
      onMouseEnter={() => setPaused(true)}
      onMouseLeave={() => setPaused(false)}
      role="region"
      aria-roledescription="carousel"
    >
      <img src={slides[index].src} alt={slides[index].alt} data-testid="slide" />

      <button onClick={() => go(index - 1)} aria-label="Previous slide">‹</button>
      <button onClick={() => go(index + 1)} aria-label="Next slide">›</button>

      <div className="dots">
        {slides.map((slide, i) => (
          <button
            key={slide.id}
            className={i === index ? 'dot active' : 'dot'}
            onClick={() => setIndex(i)}
            aria-current={i === index}
          />
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## The two bugs this archetype is built around

**1. The stale closure.**

```javascript
// BROKEN — counts to 1 and stops forever.
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => setCount(count + 1), 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []); // count is captured as 0 and never updates
```

The interval callback closed over `count` at its value on the first render — `0`. Every tick computes `0 + 1`. The fix is the functional update, `setCount(c => c + 1)`, which never reads the captured variable. Full treatment in [The Stale Closure Trap](/react-guide/7).

**2. The missing cleanup.**

```javascript
// BROKEN — a new interval every render, none ever cleared.
useEffect(() => {
  setInterval(tick, 1000);
});
```

No cleanup and no dependency array means intervals accumulate on every render. The counter appears to accelerate. Always return the cleanup function.

## Negative-modulo, the carousel gotcha

`(-1) % 5` is `-1` in JavaScript, not `4`. Going back from slide 0 gives you `slides[-1]` — undefined, and a crash. `(next + slides.length) % slides.length` normalizes it.

## Mistakes that cost points

- Reading state directly inside the interval instead of using the functional update.
- No `clearInterval` in cleanup.
- Not pausing on unmount — React warns about state updates on unmounted components.
- Restarting the timer on every render because the dependency array is wrong.
- Forgetting that [StrictMode double-invokes effects](/react-guide/33) in development, which makes a missing cleanup obvious — treat that as a free bug detector, not a nuisance.

