Five minutes of typing that fits every archetype. Get this into muscle memory and you start every project question already moving.

## The skeleton

```javascript
import { useState, useEffect, useMemo } from 'react';

// SELECTORS REQUIRED BY THE PROMPT — tick off as implemented:
// [ ] data-testid="..."
// [ ] class="..."
// [ ] id="..."

export default function App() {
  // 1. Raw data + request lifecycle
  const [data, setData] = useState([]);
  const [status, setStatus] = useState('loading'); // loading | error | ready

  // 2. User input / control state
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');

  // 3. Load (delete this block if data arrives as a prop)
  useEffect(() => {
    const controller = new AbortController();
    (async () => {
      try {
        const res = await fetch(URL, { signal: controller.signal });
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error('HTTP ' + res.status);
        setData(await res.json());
        setStatus('ready');
      } catch (err) {
        if (err.name !== 'AbortError') setStatus('error');
      }
    })();
    return () => controller.abort();
  }, []);

  // 4. Derived view — everything the UI shows is computed from 1 + 2
  const visible = useMemo(() => {
    return data; // filter / sort / paginate here
  }, [data, query]);

  // 5. The three states, before the happy path
  if (status === 'loading') return <p>Loading…</p>;
  if (status === 'error') return <p>Something went wrong.</p>;

  return (
    <div className="app">
      {/* controls */}

      {visible.length === 0 ? (
        <p>No results.</p>
      ) : (
        <ul>
          {visible.map((item) => (
            <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Why this shape

The numbered sections map onto the six things [every archetype is graded on](/test-prep/6). By typing the skeleton you have already handled loading, error, empty, and keys before writing a single feature — which is exactly the set people run out of time for and lose points on.

The selector checklist at the top costs ten seconds and prevents the single most expensive mistake on the test.

## Adapting it

| Archetype | What changes |
|---|---|
| Fetch + filter | Fill in section 4 with the filter predicate; add a debounce effect |
| Table | Section 4 becomes sort → slice; add `sortKey`, `sortDir`, `page` to section 2 |
| Form | Delete section 3; section 1 becomes `values`; section 4 becomes `validate(values)` |
| Widget | Delete section 3; section 1 is the single source of truth |
| Timer | Section 3 becomes the interval effect with cleanup |

## The vanilla-JS version

If the starter has no framework:

```javascript
const state = { data: [], query: '', status: 'loading' };
const root = document.getElementById('root');

function setState(patch) {
  Object.assign(state, patch);
  render();
}

function render() {
  if (state.status === 'loading') { root.textContent = 'Loading…'; return; }
  if (state.status === 'error') { root.textContent = 'Something went wrong.'; return; }

  const visible = state.data.filter((d) =>
    d.name.toLowerCase().includes(state.query.toLowerCase())
  );

  if (visible.length === 0) {
    root.textContent = 'No results.';
    return;
  }

  const list = document.createElement('ul');
  visible.forEach((d) => {
    const li = document.createElement('li');
    li.textContent = d.name;
    list.append(li);
  });
  root.replaceChildren(list);
}

render();
```

